<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Ituraea</h1><p class="st-meta">115 BCE – 20 BCE (approx.) · capital Chalcis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ituraea, an Arab principality of the Beqaa valley and Mount Lebanon, known for its archers and its brigandage.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Ituraeans, an Arab people, took control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beqaa-valley">Beqaa</a> valley and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-hermon">Hermon</a> in the late 2nd century BCE, as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> declined. Their ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-son-of-mennaeus">Ptolemy son of Mennaeus</a> governed from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chalcis">Chalcis</a> and extended power towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and the coast.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ituraean rulers were also high priests of a local cult, and their warriors were renowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> later serving in Roman armies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> describes them as brigands raiding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a>. Ptolemy made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> in 64 BCE and kept his principality as a Roman client.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Ptolemy's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysanias-of-chalcis">Lysanias</a> was executed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> in 36 BCE. Augustus took lands from the tetrarch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenodorus">Zenodorus</a> and gave them to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/herodian-kingdom-of-judea">Herodian Kingdom of Judea</a> in 20 BCE, and Ituraea was divided among neighbours.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ituraea.jpg" alt="Ituraea" width="320" height="538"><figcaption>Image: Claude Reignier Conder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1889_Palestine_in_the_beginning_of_the_Christian_Era.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>115 BCE – 20 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chalcis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ptolemy son of Mennaeus, Lysanias, Zenodorus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Semitic religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Aramaic, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ituraean Tetrarchy, Ituraean principality</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-68&amp;focus=ituraea" aria-label="Ituraea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5596 941 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ituraea lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5917 1211h0M6140 1232h0M5304 942h0M5908 1277h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5916 1197h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5916 1197h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Lebanon. <a href="/atlas?year=-68&amp;focus=ituraea">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
